    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii.jpg" alt="Pope Urban II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii">Pope Urban II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urbano_II_in_consacrazione_de_l%27altare_del_monastero_di_Cluny_(detail)_(crop).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Jerusalem was created by the First Crusade, launched after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii">Pope Urban II</a>’s appeal at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clermont-ferrand">Clermont</a> in 1095. After taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> in 1098, the crusaders reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, then held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a>, and stormed it on 15 July 1099, massacring much of its Muslim and Jewish population. The leaders chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey of Bouillon</a>, duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-lower-lorraine">Lower Lorraine</a>, to govern the city; he declined the title of king and was known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/advocate-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Advocate of the Holy Sepulchre</a>. In August 1099 he defeated a Fatimid relief army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ascalon">Battle of Ascalon</a>, securing the conquest. When Godfrey died in July 1100, his brother Baldwin, count of Edessa, came south and was crowned King Baldwin I at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a> on Christmas Day 1100. Baldwin I spent his reign fighting the Fatimids and extending the kingdom along the coast with the help of Genoese, Pisan and Venetian fleets, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsuf">Arsuf</a> and Caesarea in 1101, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> in 1104 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> and Sidon in 1110, and pushing south-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea">Dead Sea</a>, where he built the castle of Montréal in 1115. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> fell in 1124 under his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-ii-margrave-of-flanders">Baldwin II</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a>, the last Fatimid outpost on the coast, in 1153. The other crusader states, the County of Edessa, the Principality of Antioch and the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a>, looked to the king of Jerusalem as their leader.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jerusalem.jpg" alt="Jerusalem" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_Mount_(Aerial_view,_2007)_07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Godot13, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> settlers were called, were a minority in the kingdom. Most of the population consisted of Muslim peasants and Eastern Christians, including Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>, Armenian and other communities, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a>. The Franks lived mainly in the towns, but archaeological work has shown that many also settled in rural villages, especially in areas with Christian populations. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Church</a> established a patriarch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and filled the bishoprics with Western clergy, while pilgrimage to the holy places brought money and visitors every year. The economy combined agriculture, notably cereals, olives, vines and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane grown around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, with long-distance trade. Acre became the kingdom’s greatest port and one of the busiest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, where goods from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and the East met Italian shipping. In return for naval help, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> received quarters in the coastal cities with their own churches, courts and markets; under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pactum-warmundi">Pactum Warmundi</a> of 1123, Venice was promised a third of Tyre. The kingdom minted gold coins imitating Fatimid dinars, with Arabic inscriptions, for use in regional trade. Daily life involved constant contact across religious lines, recorded by the Syrian Muslim nobleman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usama-ibn-munqidh">Usama ibn Munqidh</a>, though for long periods Muslims and Jews were barred from living in Jerusalem itself.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-montgisard"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-montgisard.jpg" alt="Battle of Montgisard" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-montgisard">Battle of Montgisard</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schlacht_von_Montgisard_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Charles-Philippe Larivière, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom’s army was small. Its core consisted of knights owing feudal service; a list preserved by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-ibelin-count-of-jaffa">John of Ibelin</a> suggests about 675 knights, supplemented by mounted sergeants, infantry from the towns, light cavalry called turcopoles, and crusaders arriving from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. From the 1120s and 1130s the military orders, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a>, and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>, provided permanent garrisons and the money to maintain them. Castles were essential: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-karak">Kerak</a> and Montréal guarded the route east of the Jordan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belvoir-fortress">Belvoir</a> overlooked the Jordan valley, and in the thirteenth century the Templars built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlit-castle">Atlit</a> and the Teutonic Knights <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montfort-castle">Montfort</a>. The kingdom won important victories, including King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-iv-of-jerusalem">Baldwin IV</a>’s defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-montgisard">Battle of Montgisard</a> in 1177, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalric-i-of-jerusalem">Amalric</a> I invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> several times between 1163 and 1169, briefly forcing it to pay tribute. But it could not afford to lose a field army. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> on 4 July 1187, Saladin surrounded and destroyed the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">King Guy</a> of Lusignan, perhaps 20,000 men, cut off from water near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-galilee">Lake Tiberias</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> surrendered in October 1187. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a>’s victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsuf">Arsuf</a> in 1191 helped restore the coast, but another field army was destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmians">Khwarazmians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-la-forbie">La Forbie</a> in 1244.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/assizes-of-jerusalem"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/assizes-of-jerusalem.jpg" alt="Assizes of Jerusalem" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/assizes-of-jerusalem">Assizes of Jerusalem</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blason_Royaume_de_J%C3%A9rusalem.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom was a feudal monarchy in which the king’s power was limited by his barons. Kings were usually crowned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a>, and accession required recognition by the High Court, the assembly of the king’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, which also judged disputes among nobles and advised on war and law. A separate Court of the Burgesses dealt with the affairs of non-noble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, and local courts handled the business of Eastern Christians and Muslims. The great lordships, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a>, Sidon and Oultrejordain, held extensive rights over their lands and subjects. The crown passed several times through women: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melisende-queen-of-jerusalem">Melisende</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-ii-margrave-of-flanders">Baldwin II</a>, ruled with her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulk-king-of-jerusalem">Fulk of Anjou</a> from 1131 and remained powerful after his death, and the claims of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sibylla-queen-of-jerusalem">Sibylla</a>, Isabella I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-ii-of-jerusalem">Isabella II</a> brought in husbands from the West. Factional struggles weakened the monarchy at critical moments, especially during the reign of the leper king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-iv-of-jerusalem">Baldwin IV</a> (1174–1185). In the thirteenth century the kings were often absent, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> heirs ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the baronial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ibelin">Ibelin family</a> led resistance to imperial officials. Jurists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-ibelin-count-of-jaffa">John of Ibelin</a>, count of Jaffa, wrote legal treatises later known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assizes-of-jerusalem">Assizes of Jerusalem</a>, which set out the customs of the High Court.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre.jpg" alt="Church of the Holy Sepulchre" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Church_of_the_Holy_Sepulchre_by_Gerd_Eichmann_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gerd Eichmann, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Jerusalem was the centre of the crusading movement for nearly two centuries. Its foundation inspired repeated expeditions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusades</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a>. The military orders created to defend it became wealthy international institutions with estates across Europe. Italian merchant cities used their privileges in its ports to build commercial networks that outlasted the kingdom. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> builders rebuilt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a>, reconsecrated in 1149, and works such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melisende-psalter">Melisende Psalter</a> show the blending of Western, Byzantine and Eastern Christian art. William, archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, born in the kingdom, wrote one of the finest histories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. In the Muslim world the kingdom’s presence provoked a revival of the ideal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-din-zengi">Zengi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, which helped unite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The legal texts of the kingdom shaped the laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, and the title of king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> was claimed by the kings of Cyprus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and others for centuries. The memory of the crusades and of the kingdom remains a subject of debate and political rhetoric in Europe and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin.jpg" alt="Battle of Hattin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saladin_Guy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Matthew Paris, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The fall of the County of Edessa to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-din-zengi">Zengi</a> in 1144 and the failure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1148 revealed the weakness of the crusader states, while the union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> after 1169 surrounded the kingdom with a single powerful enemy. Defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a> in 1187 cost it almost all its territory, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> held out under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-of-montferrat">Conrad of Montferrat</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> in 1191 and much of the coast, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-jaffa-1192">Treaty of Jaffa</a> in 1192 confirmed a reduced kingdom with Acre as its capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> regained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> by treaty with the Ayyubid sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-kamil">al-Kamil</a> in 1229, but the city was sacked by Khwarazmian troops in 1244 and never recovered. Divided by baronial quarrels and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-saint-sabas">War of Saint Sabas</a> (1256–1258) between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, the kingdom faced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>, which defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut">Ain Jalut</a> in 1260. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> took Caesarea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsuf">Arsuf</a> in 1265, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safed">Safed</a> in 1266 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> in 1268. After a siege of about six weeks, al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ashraf-khalil">Ashraf Khalil</a> stormed Acre on 18 May 1291, and the remaining Frankish towns, including Tyre, Sidon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlit-castle">Atlit</a>, were abandoned within months. The kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> continued to bear the title.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of Jerusalem, born of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>, was the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Christian state in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> for almost two centuries, drawing repeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusades</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, enriching Italian merchants and the military orders, and provoking the Muslim revival that culminated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>’s victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>.</em></p>

